  "longest_pause": 0.46,
  "text": "Never put your real name on your bank account. The second you do, you've handed the system a map to everything you own. The wealthiest families understood this generations ago and became invisible to the people who wanted their money. The moment your money sits in your name, the taxman can see it, creditors can freeze it, and one judgment can wipe it out overnight. So what do you do instead? Step one, go to the IRS website and apply for an EIN, and employer identification number. Step 2. It asks what type of entity. Pick trust. Then revocable trust. List yourself as the grantor and the trustee. That means you still control every dollar. Step 3. Take that EIN to any bank and open an account not in your name but in the trust's name. The money is still yours. You move it. You spend it. You invest it however you want, but on paper it doesn't exist in your name. Creditors can't find it, the taxman can't freeze it, and a lawsuit can't touch it, is it legal? The system wrote the rules and the wealthiest families simply learned to use them. That's exactly how every wealthy dynasty built generational wealth, the system can't touch. They own nothing on paper and control everything in reality. Most Most people leave every dollar exposed under their own name without realizing that every visible asset is a target, waiting for someone with a lawyer to come and take it. The rich don't hide money, they structure it, and there's a massive difference between the two. If you're ready to stop being visible to every creditor and every lawsuit and start structuring your money the way the wealthiest families have done before, then you're ready to go.",
